    },
    "description": "The Meridian application gateway fronts three APIs: orders, invoices, and customer profiles. Each request must carry an authenticated principal, and the Policy Service must return a rule that permits the requested method, route, and tenant before the gateway forwards it. Recently added endpoints have been exposed when no matching rule was found, so the new requirement is that any route without an explicit grant, including unknown methods and newly introduced resources, receives no upstream access. A missing identity, an unavailable Policy Service, an unsupported token format, or an exception while evaluating a rule must also produce a rejection rather than forwarding the request. Public product documentation is served by a separate listener whose small set of routes is explicitly listed, while customer and invoice traffic never uses that listener. The gateway must record the reason for each rejection without logging credentials or sensitive invoice fields.",
    "decision_statement": "Should the gateway reject every request that lacks an explicit matching grant, including unmatched routes, invalid identities, and policy-evaluation failures?",
    "notes": "The gateway rejects every request without an explicit matching grant, including evaluation failures (fail-safe-defaults); least-privilege is the near-miss via the narrowly permitted route surface."
  },
